.SH NAME
chmod \- change mode of file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B int chmod
.I (name, mode)
.br
.B char
.I *name;
.br
.B int 
.I mode;
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file 
given in
.I name
has its mode changed to
.IR mode .
Modes are constructed by logically ORing the following
octal values:
.PP
.RS
 04000 set user ID on execution
 02000 set group ID on execution
 01000 save text image after execution
 00400 read by owner
 00200 write by owner
 00100 execute (search on directory) by owner
 00070 read, write, execute (search) by group
 00007 read, write, execute (search) by others
.RE
.PP
If an executable file has separate address spaces
(\fB\-i\fR option of
.BR ld (1)), 
mode 1000 prevents the system from
abandoning the swap-space text image 
when its last user
terminates.
When the next user executes the file, 
the text is not  read from the file
system; it is simply swapped in,
saving time.
The ability to set this bit is restricted to the super-user
because swap space is consumed
by the images.
It is recommended only for heavily used commands.
.PP
Only the owner of a file or the super-user can change the mode.
Only the super-user can set the 1000 mode.
.SH SEE ALSO
chmod(1), chown(2), mknod(2).
.SH DIAGNOSTIC
.B Chmod
fails and the file mode remains unchanged
if one or more of the following are true:
.IP
A component of
the
path prefix
is not a directory.
.SM
\%[ENOTDIR]
.IP
The named file does not exist.
.SM
\%[ENOENT]
.IP
Search permission is denied on a
component of the
path prefix.
.SM
\%[EACCES]
.IP
The effective user
.SM ID
does not match the owner of the file
and 
.SM ID
is not super-user.
.SM
\%[EPERM]
.IP
The named file resides on a read-only file system.
.SM
\%[EROFS]
.IP
.I Name
points outside the process's allocated address space.
.SM
\%[EFAULT]
.SH RETURN VALUE
A successful completion returns a value of 0. 
Otherwise, a value of \-1 is returned and
.B errno
is set.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.nf
.na
	CONSTANT CHMOD	:= 15
	...		!* filename, mode in r0, r1 respectively *!
			!* (if segmented: filename, mode in rr0, r2) *!
	clr	r4
	sc	#CHMOD
	...		!* return value in r4 *!
	   		!* carry flag set if error *!
.fi
.ad
